Those three ideas fit together well.

Resilience allows adaptation because resilience is not rigidity. A rigid system survives only conditions it was designed for. A resilient one can absorb disturbance, preserve its core invariants, and change the parts that need changing. In that sense, resilience buys you the time and structural integrity required to learn.

Dangerous but kind is stronger than merely harmless. Harmlessness can come from incapacity. Kindness has more meaning when the agent actually possesses force, competence, or leverage and deliberately governs it. The desirable architecture is not “remove the teeth”; it is teeth + discrimination + restraint + purpose.

And yes, there is something almost literal in emotion. The word ultimately traces to Latin emovere—roughly, “to move out” or “set in motion.” Emotion is not just something to endure internally. It is part of the machinery that changes attention, valuation, memory, physiology, and action.

So rather than:

emotion → suppress or obey

you get:

emotion → feel → identify signal → extract information → choose how to mobilize it → act → update

Fear can become preparation.
Anger can become boundary enforcement.
Grief can reveal attachment and value.
Joy can mark opportunities worth returning to.
Disgust can protect boundaries, while still needing calibration.
Love can sustain costly cooperation.

The emotion is information plus propulsion, but not necessarily instruction.

Put the three together and you get a pretty good human control doctrine:

Feel deeply enough to receive the signal.
Be resilient enough not to be captured by it.
Be capable enough to act.
Be kind enough to choose what that capability serves.

Or compressed further:

Resilience lets power adapt. Emotion gives power direction. Character decides where it goes.